==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|trecherie}}, from {{der|en|fro|tricherie}}, {{m|fro|trecherie}}, from {{m|fro|tricher}}, {{m|fro|trichier||to cheat}}, from {{der|en|dum|trek||a trick}}, from {{m|dum|trekken||to draw, play a trick on}}. More at [[trick]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtɹɛtʃəɹi/|/ˈtɹɛtʃɹi/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-treachery.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Deliberate, often [[calculated]], disregard for [[trust]] or [[faith]].
# The act of [[violate|violating]] the [[confidence]] of another, usually for personal [[gain]].
# [[treason|Treason]].
# {{lb|en|countable}} An act or instance of treachery.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1938|author=Norman Lindsay|authorlink=Norman Lindsay|title=Age of Consent|publisher=Ure Smith|location=Sydney|year_published=1962|page=78|passage=These submerged '''treacheries''' left an atmosphere. Even two such practised obliterators of their species as Bradly and Podson could not fail to note that each was secreting a certain reservation of opinion on the other.}}

